  "first_content_s": 3.13,
  "seconds": 28.55,
  "text": "Tides are primarily driven by the Moon\u2019s gravitational pull on Earth\u2019s oceans, with the Sun contributing about half as much. As the Moon orbits Earth, its gravity pulls water toward it, creating a bulge on the near side. A corresponding bulge forms on the far side due to centrifugal force from Earth-Moon rotation. These bulges represent high tide; between them are low tides as water recedes.\n\nCoastal locations experience tides based on their position relative to these bulges. Most coastlines see two high and two low tides daily (semi-diurnal), but some have mixed or diurnal patterns. Local geography\u2014bay shape, depth, and resonance\u2014modifies timing and height. While the basic mechanism is gravitational, precise prediction requires accounting for Earth\u2019s rotation, ocean basins, and astronomical cycles.",
  "usage": null
 },
